  "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e8{e}^{2t},{e}^{3t}\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=\u27e8{e}^{2},{e}^{3}\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1)}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3(1)}+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e8{e}^{2},{e}^{3}\u27e9 & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3}+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=\u27e8\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}\u27e9\\,.\n$$",
